From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 17:07:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebPlayer: play web apps through a hosted web browser

Streaming services and broadcasters increasingly offer their catalogue
only as a web app made for TV sets. This adds a player for such apps.

CWebClientManager runs the Wayland host for Kodi's lifetime and a web
browser, set by web.clientcommand, as one of its clients; the browser
starts with Kodi, as web.preload asks, only when a web app is installed
and the command exists. When a web page is played, CWebPlayer asks the
browser for a view of the item's profile, loads the URL, and passes the
frames the browser commits through Kodi's video renderers, so that
Kodi's OSD and dialogs compose over the page as over any other video.
While the web window is in front, keys the keymap leaves to the page and
the pointer go to the browser; text fields open Kodi's keyboard along
the bottom of the screen. The browser's audio plays through Kodi's audio
engine over a socket of its own, pages decrypt with the CDM
inputstream.adaptive installed, and the media state a page reports
drives the screensaver and display power rules as a video or music
would. The view lives as long as the player: leaving the app, from the
page or by starting another one, closes it, so that no page keeps
running in the background. The browser caches what pages download in
memory, and on disk in Kodi's profile only when web.diskcache asks for
it, since that writes to the storage as pages load.

The frames are DRM PRIME buffers, which the GBM and Wayland windowing
show, so the web host runs only there; System.SupportsWebApps tells
skins whether it does. Web apps run at the screen's resolution and
follow its mode.

On GBM with atomic DRM, the browser's video sinks hand each decoded
DMA-BUF frame over a second socket, kodi-web-video, when it is due. The
web player shows those frames on the video plane and the page on a plane
between the video and the GUI plane, where the page covers the video
except where it is transparent, so the GPU composes neither the video
nor the page. Page and video buffers prefer a plane, which keeps this
independent of the renderer the settings pick for Kodi's own video. A
page with an opaque background leaves a transparent hole where its video
element is, and the sink reports that rectangle, so the video plane
moves there, fitted to the video's aspect ratio. A frame goes back to
its sink once no plane shows it, and when a page's video stops, the page
returns to the video plane.

Web apps are a new add-on type, kodi.webapp: a manifest names the start
URL, user agent, user scripts and content filters of the app, whose
profile is the add-on id, and the viewport a TV edition is written for,
1920x1080 or 1280x720 as a television's browser has at any panel
resolution, which the page is zoomed to fit. Web apps are listed as an
add-on source and in a "Web apps" section of Estuary's home menu, accept
URLs as assets, and may let the user choose among several start URLs.
The web player plays web apps only, which ask for it by name.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
